  {
   "id": "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions",
   "title": "Inverse trigonometric integrals and volumes of revolution",
   "minutes": 25,
   "programRef": "Cameroon GCE Board — Advanced Level Further Mathematics syllabus (Upper Sixth part; scope to be checked against the official text)",
   "status": "draft",
   "author": "CastBridge (AI draft)",
   "source": "Original writing following the Cameroonian curriculum; to be reviewed by a teacher",
   "objectives": [
    "Differentiate $\\text{arcsin}\\, x$ and $\\text{arctan}\\, x$",
    "Integrate expressions giving $\\text{arcsin}\\,$ and $\\text{arctan}\\,$",
    "Find volumes of revolution about the x-axis"
   ],
   "prerequisites": [],
   "exercises": [
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e07",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e08",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e09",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e10",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e11",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-ext-e12"
   ],
   "selfCheck": [
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions-q1",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions-q2",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions-q3",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions-q4",
    "gceal-further-maths-calculus-extensions-q5"
   ],
   "reviewNotes": [
    "Whether this topic is on the Cameroon GCE Board Further Mathematics syllabus is to be confirmed: it is standard A-Level Further Maths content.",
    "Volumes of revolution may be treated in Pure Mathematics in the Cameroon syllabus; kept here for completeness. Arc length and surface area are not covered.",
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Derivatives",
     "md": "$\\frac{d}{dx}\\text{arcsin}\\, x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ for $|x|<1$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}\\text{arctan}\\, x=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}$. With a constant $a>0$: $\\frac{d}{dx}\\text{arctan}\\,\\frac xa=\\frac{a}{a^2+x^2}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Standard integrals",
     "md": "$\\int\\frac{dx}{\\sqrt{a^2-x^2}}=\\text{arcsin}\\,\\frac xa+C$ and $\\int\\frac{dx}{a^2+x^2}=\\frac1a\\text{arctan}\\,\\frac xa+C$. For a quadratic denominator complete the square first: $x^2+2x+2=(x+1)^2+1$, so $\\int\\frac{dx}{x^2+2x+2}=\\text{arctan}\\,(x+1)+C$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Volume of revolution",
     "md": "Rotating the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ through $360\\degree$ about the x-axis gives volume $V=\\pi\\int_a^by^2\\,dx$. (Think of thin discs of area $\\pi y^2$.)"
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "Forgetting the factor $\\frac1a$ in the arctan integral. Using $\\text{arcsin}\\,$ for $\\frac{1}{a^2+x^2}$ (it is arctan) or the reverse. Forgetting to square $y$ in the volume formula and to include $\\pi$. Angles from arcsin/arctan are in radians."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Evaluate $\\int_0^1\\frac{1}{x^2+2x+2}dx$.",
     "steps": [
      "$x^2+2x+2=(x+1)^2+1$: the integral is $[\\text{arctan}\\,(x+1)]_0^1$.",
      "$=\\text{arctan}\\,2-\\text{arctan}\\,1=1.1071-0.7854=0.3218$."
     ],
     "answer": "$\\text{arctan}\\,2-\\frac\\pi4\\approx0.322$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "The curve $y=\\sqrt x$ for $0\\leq x\\leq4$ is rotated about the x-axis. Find the volume.",
     "steps": [
      "$V=\\pi\\int_0^4(\\sqrt x)^2dx=\\pi\\int_0^4x\\,dx$.",
      "$=\\pi\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^4=8\\pi$."
     ],
     "answer": "$8\\pi\\approx25.1$ cubic units",
